1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,505
Two notebooks from this lecture,
run by the instructors:

2
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:06,381
the same data pipeline,

3
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:10,539
the same 1,156 cheese photos,
the same five models.

4
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:16,363
Notebook A crowns the fine-tuned MobileNet,
at 68.97 percent.

5
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,473
Notebook B crowns the frozen MobileNet,

6
00:00:20,473 --> 00:00:26,304
also at 68.97 percent,
and puts the fine-tuned one at 62.93.

7
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:29,729
So which recipe is better,
fine-tuning or freezing?

8
00:00:30,049 --> 00:00:33,569
And from these two tables, can you even tell
what was different?

9
00:00:33,889 --> 00:00:36,811
One clue is visible:
look at the epochs column.

10
00:00:37,131 --> 00:00:38,411
Hold that question.

11
00:00:38,731 --> 00:00:40,751
Part of the answer is in the table,

12
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:43,809
and part of it is in what the table never
wrote down.

13
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:46,781
Then the fine-tuned MobileNet leaves Colab.

14
00:00:47,101 --> 00:00:49,588
It gets packed into a 4 kilobyte tarball

15
00:00:49,588 --> 00:00:54,375
and trains on a machine you rent
for the length of one job, from one command.

16
00:00:54,695 --> 00:00:57,170
That command carries your W&B key.

17
00:00:57,417 --> 00:01:02,149
To follow along you need two
Colab copies with a free T4 GPU,

18
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:07,657
a free account on W&B, Docker,
and a Google Cloud project with billing.

19
00:01:07,977 --> 00:01:13,993
Vertex AI jobs cost money while they run;
gold tags mark each point, without amounts.

20
00:01:14,313 --> 00:01:18,857
A yellow tag means pause and run it,
and the study guide has every command.

21
00:01:19,177 --> 00:01:23,678
Today's two words, from the lecture:
reproducibility and accountability.

22
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:28,452
The lecture starts from three levers
for better deep learning:

23
00:01:28,452 --> 00:01:31,224
more data, better models,
and faster hardware.

24
00:01:31,544 --> 00:01:34,338
Today we pull the third one:
you rent hardware.

25
00:01:34,658 --> 00:01:37,368
And you add the bookkeeping that shows
whether it helped.

26
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:39,252
On the Cheese App roadmap,

27
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:43,718
the dashed box around experiment tracking
and baseline models is today.

28
00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:46,726
Your third milestone asks for exactly this.

29
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:49,250
Where does each piece of an experiment go?

30
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:54,017
Code to GitHub,
data to the versioning from lecture 11,

31
00:01:54,017 --> 00:01:58,060
and the environment
to pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

32
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:01,047
Weights, metrics and logs have no home yet.

33
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:04,138
A complete run should keep seven things:

34
00:02:04,138 --> 00:02:08,364
environment, code, data, model,
metrics, artifacts and notes.

35
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:13,976
The course uses Weights & Biases,
W&B for short, which is hosted for you.

36
00:02:14,296 --> 00:02:18,307
MLflow is the usual choice
if you must host it yourself.

37
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:19,659
Here is the map.

38
00:02:19,979 --> 00:02:24,966
On top, the Colab lane:
notebook A fills a folder and uploads it,

39
00:02:24,966 --> 00:02:27,958
and notebook B streams its runs to W&B.

40
00:02:28,278 --> 00:02:32,257
Below, the repo lane:
your laptop packs the code,

41
00:02:32,257 --> 00:02:37,537
a bucket holds it, Vertex AI runs it,
and the run reports to W&B.

42
00:02:37,857 --> 00:02:39,265
Today we light both.

43
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,842
Open notebook A from the course page,
save a copy,

44
00:02:43,842 --> 00:02:47,596
and switch the runtime to a GPU;
pause and set it up.

45
00:02:47,916 --> 00:02:52,311
Its stored outputs show the instructors' run
on one Tesla T4.

46
00:02:53,667 --> 00:02:55,606
Run the dataset cells:

47
00:02:55,606 --> 00:03:01,070
1,156 photos from one GitHub release,
with four cheese labels.

48
00:03:01,390 --> 00:03:02,670
Remember their order.

49
00:03:02,990 --> 00:03:09,987
Then the split: 936 to train,
104 to validate, 116 to test.

50
00:03:10,307 --> 00:03:11,565
Pause and run them.

51
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:13,144
Something to ponder.

52
00:03:13,464 --> 00:03:22,253
The cell sets validation_percent to 0.2,
yet validation is 104 out of 1,156.

53
00:03:22,573 --> 00:03:24,323
What did the code actually do?

54
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:35,130
Both splits use test_percent.

55
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:39,840
10 percent of 1,156 is 116 for testing,

56
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:47,268
then 10 percent of the remaining 1,040
is 104, the validation set.

57
00:03:47,588 --> 00:03:50,447
A small slip, copied into the repo trainer.

58
00:03:50,683 --> 00:03:52,240
Now the home-made tracker.

59
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:56,592
Cell 31 makes a folder named
after the current Unix time.

60
00:03:56,912 --> 00:04:01,816
After each model trains, the helper
from cell 12 drops five files into it:

61
00:04:01,816 --> 00:04:06,256
the model, its weights, its structure,
its history and its metrics.

62
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:08,582
Pause and train the five models.

63
00:04:09,367 --> 00:04:12,913
Cell 54 gathers the metric files
into one table,

64
00:04:12,913 --> 00:04:16,459
and cell 56 picks its top row
as the best model;

65
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:19,266
pause and run through the predictions.

66
00:04:19,586 --> 00:04:23,713
A small slip:
accuracy becomes text before sorting,

67
00:04:23,713 --> 00:04:25,332
so it sorts strings;

68
00:04:25,332 --> 00:04:28,524
9.5 percent would rank above 68 percent.

69
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:30,260
Here is a first answer.

70
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:36,191
The folder keeps settings and scores,
but not which 116 photos were the test set.

71
00:04:36,511 --> 00:04:41,460
The split has no seed,
so every run is graded on a different 116.

72
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:44,326
One photo is worth 0.86 points,

73
00:04:44,326 --> 00:04:48,351
and the winner
in notebook A leads by two photos.

74
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:55,640
Sign in with cell 62, put your own project
and bucket into cell 63, and run cell 66.

75
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:57,549
Any bucket of yours works;

76
00:04:57,549 --> 00:04:58,848
if you have none yet,

77
00:04:58,848 --> 00:05:02,253
skip this upload and come back
after the setup chapter.

78
00:05:02,573 --> 00:05:03,725
Pause and run it.

79
00:05:04,045 --> 00:05:07,579
A gold tag:
those files now sit in your bucket,

80
00:05:07,579 --> 00:05:11,640
10 to 104 megabytes of weights per model,
plus a copy.

81
00:05:12,683 --> 00:05:14,752
Notebook B adds W&B.

82
00:05:15,072 --> 00:05:19,638
Its first cell installs it,
and the -U flag means whatever is newest.

83
00:05:19,958 --> 00:05:27,223
An extra check in our signed-out stand-in
shows 0.31.0 at the time of recording,

84
00:05:27,223 --> 00:05:30,219
while the stored run used 0.22.2.

85
00:05:30,539 --> 00:05:31,691
Pause and run it.

86
00:05:31,933 --> 00:05:35,933
The data cells, through cell 32,
match notebook A,

87
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:41,213
but one also downloads a zip
of about 510 megabytes you never use.

88
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:44,413
You may delete that line;
then pause and run them.

89
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:48,703
Copy your API key
from the user settings on W&B.

90
00:05:49,023 --> 00:05:54,336
In Colab, open the key icon
and add a secret named exactly as on screen,

91
00:05:54,336 --> 00:05:56,106
with notebook access on.

92
00:05:56,426 --> 00:05:57,813
Pause and add yours.

93
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:00,074
Cell 34 signs in with it.

94
00:06:00,316 --> 00:06:02,812
Now pause and run the five training cells.

95
00:06:03,132 --> 00:06:07,548
Each starts its run with wandb.init
in one project, cheese-app-demo.

96
00:06:07,868 --> 00:06:12,690
In the ledger, the five rows
become five runs on one shared epoch axis.

97
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:15,216
But only the 2_block run shows curves.

98
00:06:15,536 --> 00:06:18,138
Compare its model.fit call
with another cell's.

99
00:06:18,458 --> 00:06:21,146
Something to ponder:
what do the other four lack?

100
00:06:27,983 --> 00:06:31,695
A callback:
code that model.fit calls after every epoch.

101
00:06:32,015 --> 00:06:35,604
Only the 2_block cell
passes the W&B callback,

102
00:06:35,604 --> 00:06:39,738
so only its loss and accuracy arrive,
epoch by epoch.

103
00:06:40,058 --> 00:06:43,099
Without one,
wandb.init gives you system charts,

104
00:06:43,099 --> 00:06:44,239
never your curves.

105
00:06:44,483 --> 00:06:48,920
Both MobileNet runs share one name,
so tell them apart by run ID.

106
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:54,766
No cell finishes the last run,
so add wandb.finish after cell 52, an extra.

107
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:59,105
Cell 55 builds the second table
from the opening.

108
00:06:59,425 --> 00:07:04,262
The fine-tuned MobileNet trained 10 epochs
here and 15 in notebook A:

109
00:07:04,262 --> 00:07:05,804
not a fair comparison.

110
00:07:06,124 --> 00:07:12,343
Now the frozen MobileNet,
with the same settings and epochs:

111
00:07:12,343 --> 00:07:15,660
67.24 in notebook A, 68.97 here.

112
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,268
Two photos apart by chance alone:
the size of the winning margin.

113
00:07:20,516 --> 00:07:27,016
To upload, edit the bucket and project
inside cell 13 and run cell 13 again,

114
00:07:27,016 --> 00:07:30,607
then run cells 64 and 66; pause and do it.

115
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:39,715
The lecture shows the pattern as three lines
around unchanged training code.

116
00:07:40,035 --> 00:07:41,977
Start the run with wandb.init.

117
00:07:42,297 --> 00:07:45,454
Watch the model
with a callback inside model.fit.

118
00:07:45,774 --> 00:07:47,715
And finish the run when training ends.

119
00:07:47,950 --> 00:07:49,806
Now notebook B's real cell.

120
00:07:50,126 --> 00:07:54,093
It starts the run,
then assigns a dictionary to wandb.config,

121
00:07:54,093 --> 00:07:56,825
renames the run, and calls wandb.run.save.

122
00:07:57,067 --> 00:07:58,411
Something to ponder.

123
00:07:58,731 --> 00:08:02,869
After that assignment,
and a later update with the execution time,

124
00:08:02,869 --> 00:08:04,875
what does the run's config hold?

125
00:08:13,717 --> 00:08:14,805
Watch the name.

126
00:08:15,125 --> 00:08:20,330
The call wandb.init points the name
wandb.config at the run's config drawer.

127
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:23,105
The assignment does not fill that drawer;

128
00:08:23,105 --> 00:08:25,920
it moves the name onto a new,
loose dictionary.

129
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:31,167
The update then writes into the loose one,
and the run finishes with its drawer empty.

130
00:08:31,417 --> 00:08:35,961
The proof, on today's W&B in offline mode,
as our stand-in.

131
00:08:36,281 --> 00:08:39,993
The name points at a plain dict,
and the run's config is empty.

132
00:08:40,313 --> 00:08:43,953
The fix passes the dictionary
to wandb.init itself,

133
00:08:43,953 --> 00:08:48,163
and the config holds all four keys
plus the execution time.

134
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:56,068
The call wandb.run.save with the word
test uploads files matching test;

135
00:08:56,068 --> 00:08:57,623
it does not save the run.

136
00:08:57,867 --> 00:09:01,737
The repo's trainer gets the first
and last lines right:

137
00:09:01,737 --> 00:09:05,184
config inside wandb.init,
then update and finish.

138
00:09:05,504 --> 00:09:07,104
But the middle line is gone.

139
00:09:07,424 --> 00:09:11,291
One commit
in October 2025 removed the callback,

140
00:09:11,291 --> 00:09:12,822
without saying why.

141
00:09:13,142 --> 00:09:17,133
So its runs get config and system charts,
and no curves;

142
00:09:17,133 --> 00:09:19,627
exercise 1 adds the callback again.

143
00:09:20,667 --> 00:09:26,086
The second half of the lecture is Vertex AI,
Google Cloud's machine learning platform.

144
00:09:26,406 --> 00:09:30,021
Three console entries matter:
Workbench for notebooks,

145
00:09:30,021 --> 00:09:33,168
Training for jobs,
and Pipelines for workflows.

146
00:09:33,488 --> 00:09:38,672
Today is Training, on the custom jobs tab;
lecture 15 uses Pipelines.

147
00:09:38,917 --> 00:09:42,906
Serverless training means you rent
the hardware only for the training step.

148
00:09:43,226 --> 00:09:46,896
Nobody sets up a server,
and billing stops when the step ends.

149
00:09:47,216 --> 00:09:50,586
There is still a machine;
you just don't manage its lifetime.

150
00:09:50,834 --> 00:09:54,173
Step one:
move the notebook code into a Python file,

151
00:09:54,173 --> 00:09:56,935
and turn its hard-coded choices into flags.

152
00:09:57,255 --> 00:10:00,693
The flags
are epochs, batch size and model name,

153
00:10:00,693 --> 00:10:03,058
plus train_base and your W&B key.

154
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:05,972
Step two packs that file into a tarball,

155
00:10:05,972 --> 00:10:09,380
one compressed archive,
and uploads it to a bucket.

156
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:15,165
Step three runs from what the lecture
calls the Model Trainer CLI Container,

157
00:10:15,165 --> 00:10:19,193
our cockpit on your laptop:
it asks Vertex AI for a job.

158
00:10:19,513 --> 00:10:24,420
Vertex AI then starts a machine,
CPU only or with one T4.

159
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:28,858
It loads Google's prebuilt TensorFlow image,
installs your tarball,

160
00:10:28,858 --> 00:10:32,484
runs your trainer with your flags,
and deletes the machine.

161
00:10:32,734 --> 00:10:35,017
Two facts drive everything after this.

162
00:10:35,337 --> 00:10:39,245
The job cannot see your laptop:
its data comes from the internet,

163
00:10:39,245 --> 00:10:42,974
and anything it should keep
must be written somewhere durable.

164
00:10:43,294 --> 00:10:45,704
And the image is Google's, not yours.

165
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:47,230
A quick prediction.

166
00:10:47,550 --> 00:10:50,878
Your laptop has no GPU and no TensorFlow.

167
00:10:51,198 --> 00:10:54,782
Which machines in this picture
will ever import TensorFlow?

168
00:10:59,617 --> 00:11:04,353
Two: the Colab runtime,
and the container inside each Vertex job.

169
00:11:04,673 --> 00:11:08,449
Never the cockpit on your laptop;
the tarball chapter proves it.

170
00:11:09,483 --> 00:11:13,472
Clone the model-training repo
and move into it; pause and run it.

171
00:11:13,792 --> 00:11:16,907
Two extras show the course pin
and the repo's files.

172
00:11:24,316 --> 00:11:27,687
In the console, enable the Vertex AI API.

173
00:11:28,007 --> 00:11:31,484
Then request GPU quota,
an allowance per region.

174
00:11:31,804 --> 00:11:37,798
Filter for custom model training,
pick T4 GPUs, include us-central1,

175
00:11:37,798 --> 00:11:41,852
where cli.sh runs,
set a new limit and submit.

176
00:11:42,172 --> 00:11:43,452
Pause and do both.

177
00:11:43,772 --> 00:11:50,108
Approval may take hours or be refused;
cli.sh runs on CPU without it.

178
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:55,512
Next, a secrets folder beside the repo,
never inside it; pause and make it.

179
00:11:58,866 --> 00:12:00,427
Then the identity:

180
00:12:00,427 --> 00:12:04,848
a service account model-trainer
with Storage Admin,

181
00:12:04,848 --> 00:12:08,780
AI Platform Admin
and Vertex AI Administrator,

182
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:13,565
and one JSON key saved
in secrets as model-trainer.json.

183
00:12:13,885 --> 00:12:15,400
Pause and create yours.

184
00:12:19,300 --> 00:12:20,537
One honest note.

185
00:12:20,857 --> 00:12:24,763
This recording machine
has no Google credentials,

186
00:12:24,763 --> 00:12:27,791
no W&B account, no GPU and no browser.

187
00:12:28,111 --> 00:12:31,900
The key in that listing
is an obviously invalid stand-in,

188
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:34,425
moved out before the container starts.

189
00:12:34,745 --> 00:12:38,470
So each cloud step shows
the failure a misplaced key gives,

190
00:12:38,470 --> 00:12:40,868
and successes come from named sources.

191
00:12:41,116 --> 00:12:44,529
Then create a bucket with your own name;
pause and create it.

192
00:12:44,849 --> 00:12:46,355
Two extras show why:

193
00:12:46,355 --> 00:12:49,894
our placeholder returns 404,
it exists nowhere,

194
00:12:49,894 --> 00:12:54,641
while the README's name returns 401,
it belongs to someone else.

195
00:12:54,961 --> 00:12:59,015
A gold tag: the bucket stores
your code and experiment folders.

196
00:12:59,250 --> 00:13:00,842
Last, copy your W&B key

197
00:13:00,842 --> 00:13:04,925
and export it in the terminal
you'll use for the container.

198
00:13:05,245 --> 00:13:06,418
Pause and do it.

199
00:13:06,738 --> 00:13:10,471
An extra echo prints its length,
40 for our placeholder.

200
00:13:10,791 --> 00:13:12,230
It must not be zero,

201
00:13:12,230 --> 00:13:15,826
and the trainer's pinned wandb
accepts exactly 40.

202
00:13:16,146 --> 00:13:18,800
If yours is a different length, keep going,

203
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:23,058
but watch the chapter Who submits,
who runs, before you submit a job.

204
00:13:24,133 --> 00:13:25,477
Something to ponder.

205
00:13:25,797 --> 00:13:29,573
You exported the key, closed that terminal,
and opened a new one.

206
00:13:29,893 --> 00:13:31,792
What does the same echo print now?

207
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:43,156
Zero: an exported variable exists only
in that shell and its children.

208
00:13:43,476 --> 00:13:46,932
Part 1 is done: the key is set,
the bucket has a name.

209
00:13:47,252 --> 00:13:50,366
Next, we build the cockpit,
and today it won't build.

210
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:57,000
Part 2 ships the trainer: build the cockpit,
pack the code, send one job.

211
00:13:57,320 --> 00:14:02,203
Put your bucket and project
on lines 8 and 9 of docker-shell.sh;

212
00:14:02,203 --> 00:14:03,805
pause and edit yours.

213
00:14:05,050 --> 00:14:09,445
A small slip: the README's copy
of this script is not the real file.

214
00:14:09,765 --> 00:14:13,467
It has notes in brackets
and an older line for Apple chips,

215
00:14:13,467 --> 00:14:15,098
so edit the script itself.

216
00:14:15,418 --> 00:14:19,557
It builds
for amd64 and mounts your code and secrets.

217
00:14:19,800 --> 00:14:23,660
Now run sh docker-shell.sh
from inside the repo;

218
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:25,027
pause and run it.

219
00:14:25,347 --> 00:14:27,672
Today it fails at step 2 of 14.

220
00:14:27,992 --> 00:14:32,774
All 22 packages return 404 Not Found
from the Debian mirror,

221
00:14:32,774 --> 00:14:35,245
and the build stops at line 18.

222
00:14:35,565 --> 00:14:40,152
The script's set -e, exit on error,
then stops it before docker run.

223
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:41,744
Something to ponder.

224
00:14:42,064 --> 00:14:46,416
Every error says 404 from the Debian mirror,
and your internet works.

225
00:14:46,736 --> 00:14:49,211
Which line of the Dockerfile
picks your Debian?

226
00:14:58,050 --> 00:14:59,565
Line 2, the FROM line.

227
00:14:59,885 --> 00:15:01,340
Bullseye is Debian 11,

228
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:06,893
and at the time of recording its security
mirror lists packages it no longer serves;

229
00:15:06,893 --> 00:15:08,546
they moved to an archive.

230
00:15:08,866 --> 00:15:13,241
The first fix, an extra,
switches the base to bookworm, Debian 12;

231
00:15:13,241 --> 00:15:14,434
pause and make it.

232
00:15:14,783 --> 00:15:16,255
Pause and run it again.

233
00:15:16,575 --> 00:15:18,415
Now it stops at step 8:

234
00:15:18,415 --> 00:15:22,222
google-cloud-sdk
has no installation candidate,

235
00:15:22,222 --> 00:15:24,895
and google-cloud-cli replaces it.

236
00:15:25,215 --> 00:15:27,178
Google renamed its apt package.

237
00:15:27,417 --> 00:15:29,882
The second fix, also an extra:

238
00:15:29,882 --> 00:15:35,140
install google-cloud-cli instead,
on line 37; pause and make it.

239
00:15:35,460 --> 00:15:38,468
An extra git diff shows
everything you changed:

240
00:15:38,468 --> 00:15:41,603
your two values, the two fixes,
and nothing else.

241
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:46,806
Pause and run it a third time:
it builds, about 5 minutes here,

242
00:15:46,806 --> 00:15:50,661
longer on Apple Silicon,
where amd64 is emulated.

243
00:15:50,981 --> 00:15:52,458
The banner says running,

244
00:15:52,458 --> 00:15:55,781
then gcloud cannot open the key file:
our missing key.

245
00:15:56,101 --> 00:15:59,130
With yours,
this line confirms the service account.

246
00:15:59,450 --> 00:16:03,887
The script carries on anyway,
because the entrypoint has no set -e.

247
00:16:04,133 --> 00:16:09,701
The architecture is x86_64, on a Mac too;
the versions are on screen.

248
00:16:10,021 --> 00:16:14,080
A small slip:
activated is printed before the activation,

249
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:15,504
a label, not a fact.

250
00:16:17,150 --> 00:16:19,774
Inside the cockpit, a few extra checks.

251
00:16:20,094 --> 00:16:22,675
/secrets is empty, our sandbox.

252
00:16:22,995 --> 00:16:28,286
Project, bucket and key came through;
gcloud, gsutil and uv are here.

253
00:16:28,606 --> 00:16:30,198
Pause and check yours:

254
00:16:30,198 --> 00:16:34,322
the last number should be 40
for this wandb pin, never 0;

255
00:16:34,322 --> 00:16:37,609
if not,
see the chapter Who submits, who runs.

256
00:16:37,929 --> 00:16:40,104
But importing TensorFlow fails:

257
00:16:40,104 --> 00:16:43,262
this container submits jobs,
it never trains.

258
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,923
Review setup.py; pause and open it.

259
00:16:49,243 --> 00:16:52,507
It is the label pip uses
to install the tarball.

260
00:16:52,827 --> 00:16:58,501
The only declared dependency is wandb,
pinned to 0.15.11.

261
00:16:58,821 --> 00:17:02,346
Its find_packages()
call finds the trainer folder

262
00:17:02,346 --> 00:17:05,438
because the folder has its own __init__.py,

263
00:17:05,438 --> 00:17:08,891
so once installed,
python -m trainer.task works.

264
00:17:09,211 --> 00:17:12,033
TensorFlow and scikit-learn
are not declared:

265
00:17:12,033 --> 00:17:15,546
they are assumed to come
from the image Google provides,

266
00:17:15,546 --> 00:17:18,619
and a later chapter shows
the tarball lacks them.

267
00:17:18,939 --> 00:17:21,947
Requests only arrives
because wandb needs it.

268
00:17:22,183 --> 00:17:25,383
Review task.py next; pause and open it.

269
00:17:25,703 --> 00:17:27,860
The notebook choices are flags now,

270
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,804
and the --wandb_key flag defaults
to the text 16: remember that.

271
00:17:32,124 --> 00:17:37,613
The data comes from one GitHub release,
since the job cannot see your laptop,

272
00:17:37,613 --> 00:17:40,679
and the W&B login runs after that download.

273
00:17:40,917 --> 00:17:45,688
Now pack it: run sh
package-trainer.sh inside the container;

274
00:17:45,688 --> 00:17:47,040
pause and run it.

275
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:50,292
Tar lists eight entries,
and the upload fails:

276
00:17:50,292 --> 00:17:52,714
our placeholder bucket does not exist.

277
00:17:53,034 --> 00:17:57,194
Line 4 uses a fixed name,
so each upload overwrites the last.

278
00:17:58,033 --> 00:18:01,553
Extra checks: the tarball is 4,016 bytes.

279
00:18:01,873 --> 00:18:06,370
On our Linux engine it belongs to root,
because the container wrote it;

280
00:18:06,370 --> 00:18:08,017
on Docker Desktop, to you.

281
00:18:08,337 --> 00:18:10,023
Git ignores it either way.

282
00:18:10,267 --> 00:18:11,611
Something to ponder.

283
00:18:11,931 --> 00:18:14,128
You fix a typo in task.py.

284
00:18:14,448 --> 00:18:18,117
Do you rebuild the image,
run the package script again, or both?

285
00:18:24,967 --> 00:18:26,503
Only the package script.

286
00:18:26,823 --> 00:18:31,644
Vertex AI runs the tarball in your bucket,
and your image never travels.

287
00:18:31,964 --> 00:18:35,676
The cockpit mounts your code folder,
so it already sees the edit.

288
00:18:35,996 --> 00:18:40,007
A gold tag: the tarball is stored
in your bucket, and it is tiny.

289
00:18:41,650 --> 00:18:44,935
Review cli.sh; pause and open it.

290
00:18:45,255 --> 00:18:47,829
It names the job with random hex,

291
00:18:47,829 --> 00:18:52,274
asks for one n1-standard-4 machine,
and sets a GPU image.

292
00:18:52,594 --> 00:18:56,955
Then it points at your tarball in the bucket
and the module to run,

293
00:18:56,955 --> 00:19:00,274
and builds the arguments,
ending with your W&B key.

294
00:19:00,517 --> 00:19:06,984
Further down, the GPU block is commented out
and line 31 swaps in the CPU image,

295
00:19:06,984 --> 00:19:09,328
so only the CPU command runs.

296
00:19:09,648 --> 00:19:14,649
A small slip: the README shows GPU first
and CPU as optional,

297
00:19:14,649 --> 00:19:17,519
but the script already runs on CPU.

298
00:19:17,767 --> 00:19:21,647
A gold tag first:
once the job's machine is provisioned,

299
00:19:21,647 --> 00:19:24,487
your project pays until the script exits.

300
00:19:24,807 --> 00:19:26,893
Check that your key length is 40;

301
00:19:26,893 --> 00:19:31,506
if not, watch the next chapter,
Who submits, who runs, before you submit.

302
00:19:31,826 --> 00:19:35,282
Now sh cli.sh; pause and run it.

303
00:19:35,602 --> 00:19:40,615
The first line is real:
the Vertex AI endpoint in us-central1.

304
00:19:40,935 --> 00:19:45,209
Then it stops with no active account,
because the key never loaded;

305
00:19:45,209 --> 00:19:47,186
an extra auth list confirms it.

306
00:19:47,506 --> 00:19:50,983
With your key,
gcloud reports the job as created.

307
00:19:51,217 --> 00:19:55,654
An extra trace with sh -x
shows the job it tried to send.

308
00:19:55,974 --> 00:20:00,113
The GPU image is set,
then overridden by the CPU image.

309
00:20:00,433 --> 00:20:01,798
Then the full command.

310
00:20:02,118 --> 00:20:05,326
The worker pool spec
is the job's machine order:

311
00:20:05,326 --> 00:20:09,670
machine type, one replica,
meaning one machine, image and module.

312
00:20:09,990 --> 00:20:14,513
Last come the arguments,
ending with your W&B key in plain text.

313
00:20:14,967 --> 00:20:16,311
Something to ponder.

314
00:20:16,631 --> 00:20:20,386
Your W&B key is now
part of the job's arguments.

315
00:20:20,706 --> 00:20:22,028
Who can see it later?

316
00:20:30,867 --> 00:20:33,498
Anyone who can view jobs in this project,

317
00:20:33,498 --> 00:20:38,760
because the job spec keeps its arguments,
plus your shell history if typed inline.

318
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:42,403
Use a dedicated key
and rotate it at the end;

319
00:20:42,403 --> 00:20:44,840
the exercises use Secret Manager.

320
00:20:45,083 --> 00:20:49,264
The other way it goes wrong:
a terminal where the key was never exported.

321
00:20:49,584 --> 00:20:54,107
Inside, its length is 0,
and the arguments end with an empty key.

322
00:20:54,427 --> 00:20:58,544
So the job would download its data,
and only then fail at login.

323
00:20:58,864 --> 00:21:00,882
Exit, export, and start again;

324
00:21:00,882 --> 00:21:04,177
first exit any old cockpit,
which holds the name.

325
00:21:05,767 --> 00:21:08,178
Three badges take part in every job.

326
00:21:08,498 --> 00:21:12,252
The first is your key file,
for the model-trainer service account.

327
00:21:12,572 --> 00:21:16,967
Its three roles let it write
to your bucket and create jobs: it submits.

328
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:21,498
The third is your W&B key,
for a different building altogether,

329
00:21:21,498 --> 00:21:24,432
carried by hand inside the job's arguments.

330
00:21:24,752 --> 00:21:26,886
The middle lanyard is still empty.

331
00:21:27,134 --> 00:21:28,478
Something to ponder.

332
00:21:28,798 --> 00:21:32,220
The job installs your package
from your private bucket,

333
00:21:32,220 --> 00:21:35,816
but your key file never left your laptop.
How?

334
00:21:44,650 --> 00:21:48,757
The second badge:
your project's Vertex AI service agent,

335
00:21:48,757 --> 00:21:51,135
a service account Google manages.

336
00:21:51,455 --> 00:21:56,405
By default the job runs as it,
and it can open buckets in the same project.

337
00:21:56,725 --> 00:21:58,794
Your key only proved who submitted.

338
00:21:59,114 --> 00:22:04,661
If a job ever gets a 403 on your bucket,
that is the identity to grant access.

339
00:22:05,300 --> 00:22:09,993
On Vertex, the job's first step installs
your tarball into Google's image.

340
00:22:10,313 --> 00:22:11,958
We can't run Vertex here,

341
00:22:11,958 --> 00:22:15,775
so we rerun that step on the laptop,
tagged as a stand-in.

342
00:22:16,095 --> 00:22:21,772
In a plain Python 3.10 container,
pip installs the tarball:

343
00:22:21,772 --> 00:22:24,948
your package, plus wandb 0.15.11.

344
00:22:25,184 --> 00:22:29,334
An extra pip list shows wandb,
requests and their helpers,

345
00:22:29,334 --> 00:22:32,053
but no TensorFlow and no scikit-learn.

346
00:22:32,373 --> 00:22:35,305
The trainer counts
on the prebuilt image for those,

347
00:22:35,305 --> 00:22:37,088
which we did not pull to check.

348
00:22:37,408 --> 00:22:40,266
Without it,
the trainer cannot even print its help.

349
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:47,547
Then the trainer's first W&B step, line 153,
with three keys.

350
00:22:47,867 --> 00:22:52,649
The empty key from the last chapter
fails with api_key not configured,

351
00:22:52,649 --> 00:22:56,043
and on Vertex that comes
after the data download,

352
00:22:56,043 --> 00:22:57,637
on billed machine time.

353
00:22:57,957 --> 00:22:59,962
The default of 16 fails:

354
00:22:59,962 --> 00:23:03,973
API key must be 40 characters long,
yours was 2.

355
00:23:04,293 --> 00:23:08,130
Our 40 character placeholder
passes the offline length check,

356
00:23:08,130 --> 00:23:09,200
and nothing more.

357
00:23:09,750 --> 00:23:15,958
One more: this old wandb pin accepts
exactly 40 characters, and 47 fails.

358
00:23:16,278 --> 00:23:20,118
We could not check
which key format W&B gives out today.

359
00:23:20,438 --> 00:23:24,641
If yours differs, this pin refuses it,
so check before you submit.

360
00:23:24,961 --> 00:23:26,937
One fix, untested here:

361
00:23:26,937 --> 00:23:31,492
raise the wandb pin in setup.py
to a current release,

362
00:23:31,492 --> 00:23:35,649
run sh package-trainer.sh again,
and then submit.

363
00:23:37,283 --> 00:23:43,896
To view jobs, open Vertex AI, then Training,
then Custom jobs; pause and look for yours.

364
00:23:44,216 --> 00:23:48,440
It is a custom job, not a training pipeline,
so look on that tab.

365
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:53,966
Open your job and follow its logs link;
wait for the line Training Job Complete.

366
00:23:54,200 --> 00:23:55,608
What will the job print?

367
00:23:55,928 --> 00:23:59,536
No job log exists for this video,
so here is its shape,

368
00:23:59,536 --> 00:24:02,029
built from the code's own print lines.

369
00:24:02,349 --> 00:24:06,104
The counts are the ones both notebooks
printed for the same code.

370
00:24:06,350 --> 00:24:08,692
The labels line has no fixed order,

371
00:24:08,692 --> 00:24:12,238
as the two notebooks show,
and no label map is saved.

372
00:24:12,558 --> 00:24:14,658
The summary ends with a stray None,

373
00:24:14,658 --> 00:24:17,358
because the code prints
what summary returns.

374
00:24:17,678 --> 00:24:20,794
Training itself is a gap here,
with no epochs,

375
00:24:20,794 --> 00:24:24,248
loss or times,
then the line Training Job Complete.

376
00:24:24,483 --> 00:24:25,678
To view metrics,

377
00:24:25,678 --> 00:24:30,606
the README sends you to the W&B
project cheese-training-vertex-ai.

378
00:24:30,926 --> 00:24:34,872
It promises training
metrics, tracked and updated automatically.

379
00:24:35,192 --> 00:24:37,411
To put it kindly, not with this code.

380
00:24:37,731 --> 00:24:42,032
From task.py, the run named
after the model gets its config,

381
00:24:42,032 --> 00:24:44,111
including the execution time,

382
00:24:44,111 --> 00:24:47,694
and system charts,
and its curve slots stay empty.

383
00:24:48,014 --> 00:24:49,912
Pause and open your project.

384
00:24:50,150 --> 00:24:53,179
Something to ponder,
with the lecture's list of seven.

385
00:24:53,499 --> 00:24:55,888
Which of them does this run actually keep?

386
00:25:04,733 --> 00:25:06,610
The model and its settings, yes.

387
00:25:06,930 --> 00:25:13,352
The environment only partly: system charts,
while the image tag sits in cli.sh.

388
00:25:13,672 --> 00:25:17,405
Metrics, none:
the execution time goes into the config.

389
00:25:17,725 --> 00:25:19,453
Logs yes, weights no.

390
00:25:19,773 --> 00:25:24,658
Code, data and notes, no: no commit,
a fixed zip, and an unseeded split.

391
00:25:24,978 --> 00:25:29,714
The folder from notebook A has no commit,
no split and no notes either.

392
00:25:30,034 --> 00:25:33,405
So the verdict:
these tables cannot crown a recipe.

393
00:25:33,725 --> 00:25:37,608
Seed the split, match the epochs,
keep the code version, then compare.

394
00:25:37,928 --> 00:25:40,274
Exercise 1 adds a small callback.

395
00:25:41,317 --> 00:25:44,799
Now the reverse
of the README's optional CPU step:

396
00:25:44,799 --> 00:25:47,376
what would it take to train on a GPU?

397
00:25:47,696 --> 00:25:49,402
Three switches must agree.

398
00:25:49,722 --> 00:25:53,221
The accelerator:
one T4 in the worker pool spec.

399
00:25:53,541 --> 00:25:58,768
The image: TensorFlow built for GPUs,
with CUDA, the GPU libraries.

400
00:25:59,088 --> 00:26:02,650
And the quota:
T4s for custom training in this region.

401
00:26:02,970 --> 00:26:05,317
The chip lights only when all three are on.

402
00:26:06,284 --> 00:26:09,697
The quota is the console request
from the setup chapter.

403
00:26:10,017 --> 00:26:13,946
An extra check shows Google
still serves both GPU images,

404
00:26:13,946 --> 00:26:15,670
at the time of recording.

405
00:26:15,917 --> 00:26:20,568
To switch, uncomment the GPU
block and comment out the CPU block.

406
00:26:20,888 --> 00:26:24,685
Leave both active,
and you submit two jobs with the same name.

407
00:26:24,934 --> 00:26:29,299
Here the GPU block would fail the same way
as our CPU submit did,

408
00:26:29,299 --> 00:26:30,864
with no active account,

409
00:26:30,864 --> 00:26:34,470
so there is nothing new to show,
and nothing we fake.

410
00:26:34,717 --> 00:26:40,498
A gold tag: a GPU adds to the bill
for as long as it is attached, used or not.

411
00:26:40,734 --> 00:26:42,078
Something to ponder.

412
00:26:42,398 --> 00:26:44,958
You add the accelerator to the CPU block.

413
00:26:45,278 --> 00:26:49,061
The job runs,
and its log says GPU Available:

414
00:26:49,061 --> 00:26:50,995
[], an empty list. Why?

415
00:26:57,834 --> 00:27:01,589
Line 31 sets a CPU image, which has no CUDA.

416
00:27:01,909 --> 00:27:04,831
The T4 is attached, idle, and still billed.

417
00:27:05,151 --> 00:27:07,284
Exercise 5 lets you try it.

418
00:27:08,333 --> 00:27:13,148
The last idea of the lecture
is multi-GPU training, in three shapes:

419
00:27:13,148 --> 00:27:14,943
one machine with one GPU,

420
00:27:14,943 --> 00:27:18,317
one machine with several,
and several machines.

421
00:27:18,637 --> 00:27:21,304
Today is the middle one,
the mirrored strategy.

422
00:27:21,624 --> 00:27:26,765
The single-GPU trainer also creates one,
but only to count GPUs.

423
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:28,259
Here is the idea.

424
00:27:28,579 --> 00:27:33,080
Each GPU holds an identical copy
of the model, called a replica.

425
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:38,755
A global batch of 64, one step's worth,
splits into two halves of 32.

426
00:27:39,075 --> 00:27:41,933
Each GPU computes gradients on its half.

427
00:27:42,253 --> 00:27:45,535
The two are averaged
across a link called all-reduce,

428
00:27:45,535 --> 00:27:49,187
and both copies take the same step,
so they stay identical.

429
00:27:49,507 --> 00:27:54,740
Variables created inside the scope
are mirrored, one synced copy per GPU;

430
00:27:54,740 --> 00:27:57,464
a model built outside is not mirrored.

431
00:27:57,700 --> 00:28:03,823
Review cli-multi-gpu.sh first,
in README order; pause and open it.

432
00:28:04,143 --> 00:28:09,732
It asks for one n1-standard-32 machine
with two T4s, for 5 epochs.

433
00:28:10,052 --> 00:28:18,222
A small slip: its TensorFlow image
is older, 2.14, where cli.sh uses 2.17.

434
00:28:18,466 --> 00:28:22,221
Then review the multi-GPU trainer;
pause and open it.

435
00:28:22,541 --> 00:28:24,717
These are the lecture's three changes.

436
00:28:25,037 --> 00:28:26,999
It creates the mirrored strategy.

437
00:28:27,319 --> 00:28:30,754
It multiplies the batch size
by the number of replicas.

438
00:28:31,074 --> 00:28:35,277
And it builds and compiles the model
inside the scope of the strategy.

439
00:28:35,597 --> 00:28:37,304
model.fit is unchanged.

440
00:28:37,550 --> 00:28:38,894
Something to ponder.

441
00:28:39,214 --> 00:28:45,166
Two GPUs, --batch_size=32,
and 936 training images.

442
00:28:45,486 --> 00:28:47,116
How many steps per epoch,

443
00:28:47,116 --> 00:28:50,179
and how many images
does each GPU see per step?

444
00:28:59,016 --> 00:29:03,953
The global batch is 64,
so 15 steps, the last one partial,

445
00:29:03,953 --> 00:29:07,357
and 32 images per GPU on each full step.

446
00:29:07,677 --> 00:29:08,829
Two caveats.

447
00:29:09,149 --> 00:29:13,821
The learning rate stays at 0.001
while the batch doubles.

448
00:29:14,141 --> 00:29:16,465
And two GPUs are not twice as fast,

449
00:29:16,465 --> 00:29:20,115
because start-up,
image pull and download don't shrink.

450
00:29:20,350 --> 00:29:22,354
You need quota for two T4s

451
00:29:22,354 --> 00:29:26,515
and 32 virtual CPUs of custom
training in your region.

452
00:29:26,835 --> 00:29:30,342
A gold tag:
this is the biggest machine of the lecture,

453
00:29:30,342 --> 00:29:32,382
so check the pricing page first.

454
00:29:32,702 --> 00:29:38,249
If your quota came through,
pause and run sh cli-multi-gpu.sh.

455
00:29:38,569 --> 00:29:42,686
Here it fails like cli.sh,
with no active account.

456
00:29:43,733 --> 00:29:49,578
One launcher the README never mentions
is cli.py, the Python SDK route.

457
00:29:49,898 --> 00:29:52,564
As an extra run, it fails at once,

458
00:29:52,564 --> 00:29:56,405
because only cli.sh sets
the GCP_REGION variable.

459
00:29:56,725 --> 00:29:59,520
Export it,
and it fails at the missing key file.

460
00:30:02,450 --> 00:30:04,178
And where is the trained model?

461
00:30:04,498 --> 00:30:07,339
When the script ends,
the machine is deleted,

462
00:30:07,339 --> 00:30:08,854
and the weights with it:

463
00:30:08,854 --> 00:30:12,711
nothing calls model.save,
and the --model-dir flag is ignored.

464
00:30:13,031 --> 00:30:18,130
Lecture 15 saves the model to a bucket;
exercise 4 does it today.

465
00:30:18,366 --> 00:30:21,310
Time to shut down;
pause and work through the list.

466
00:30:21,630 --> 00:30:24,413
From inside the cockpit, or in the console,

467
00:30:24,413 --> 00:30:27,390
make sure no custom job
is running or pending.

468
00:30:27,710 --> 00:30:30,526
Remove the tarball
and experiment folders too.

469
00:30:30,846 --> 00:30:34,302
Then exit;
an extra docker ps shows nothing left.

470
00:30:34,622 --> 00:30:39,081
Finish the last W&B run,
and disconnect the Colab runtimes.

471
00:30:39,401 --> 00:30:42,762
At the end of term,
delete the model-trainer key

472
00:30:42,762 --> 00:30:44,443
and rotate your W&B key,

473
00:30:44,443 --> 00:30:46,100
which traveled in a job.

474
00:30:46,350 --> 00:30:48,142
The whole lecture on one map.

475
00:30:48,462 --> 00:30:51,150
A run is only as useful as what it keeps.

476
00:30:51,470 --> 00:30:55,033
Curves need a callback;
config goes into wandb.init.

477
00:30:55,353 --> 00:30:58,357
Only the tarball travels,
into Google's image,

478
00:30:58,357 --> 00:31:01,753
run as the service agent,
your key in its arguments.

479
00:31:02,073 --> 00:31:04,141
One GPU needs three switches;

480
00:31:04,141 --> 00:31:07,491
two GPUs split
the batch and average gradients.

481
00:31:09,316 --> 00:31:11,321
5 questions to test yourself.

482
00:31:11,641 --> 00:31:13,924
One: what did neither table write down?

483
00:31:14,244 --> 00:31:17,828
Two: what travels to Vertex AI,
and what never does?

484
00:31:18,148 --> 00:31:20,367
Three: why is the config empty?

485
00:31:20,687 --> 00:31:24,079
Four: who submits, who runs,
and where does your key end up?

486
00:31:24,399 --> 00:31:29,391
Five: with two GPUs, what is split,
averaged, and created inside the scope?

487
00:31:29,633 --> 00:31:31,681
Answers are in the exercises.

488
00:31:32,001 --> 00:31:35,726
Next, lecture 14 serves
containers without servers,

489
00:31:35,726 --> 00:31:39,596
and lecture 15 saves this model
and chains the steps.

490
00:31:39,916 --> 00:31:43,180
Hardware rented,
and a log of what you rented it for.

